## Initial Q&A
### Did any individual in the United States successfully cast four separate, counted ballots in the 2020 election using four different ballot applications?
Answer: According to election experts, it is not possible for an individual to cast multiple votes using multiple ballot applications, as "each voter has a unique voter ID number that is tied to a single ballot." Even if a voter submits multiple applications, they would only receive a single ballot, and election officials verify signatures against voter files to ensure that "people can no more vote multiple mail ballots than they could go into various different polling places on Election Day and try to vote."

Source URL: https://www.fox29.com/news/receiving-multiple-ballot-applications-wont-let-you-vote-more-than-once-heres-why

### What are the official election security protocols in the relevant jurisdiction to prevent a voter from casting multiple ballots if that voter receives multiple ballot applications?
Answer: In Oregon, the state prevents voters from casting multiple ballots by tracking all transactions through the Oregon Centralized Voter Registration (OCVR) system, where officials must "always flag the individual voter’s file that more than one ballot was issued." If a voter attempts to return more than one ballot, protocols dictate that only one is counted; for instance, if a replacement ballot was requested and "the voter returns two ballots, count only the first ballot received" while inactivating the other. Additionally, every returned ballot must undergo signature verification against the voter's registration record, and any clear attempts to intentionally commit fraud are referred to the Secretary of State's office as a potential election law violation.

Source URL: https://sos.oregon.gov/elections/documents/vbm_manual.pdf

### Are ballot applications in the United States legally equivalent to actual ballots, or do ballot applications require verification before an official ballot is issued to a voter?
Answer: Based on the provided source, ballot applications (referred to as "Vote-by-Mail requests") are not equivalent to actual ballots and require verification before an official ballot is issued. To request a ballot, a voter must provide personal information, such as a Florida Driver's License or ID number, or the last four digits of their Social Security number, "whichever may be verified in our records."

Source URL: https://www.duvalelections.com/Vote-By-Mail

### How do voter registration systems in the United States detect and handle duplicate ballot submissions from the same registered voter?
Answer: To detect duplicate ballot submissions, election systems utilize official return envelopes that "feature unique bar codes to identify each voter." If more than one ballot is submitted for the same individual, "election officials would notice that a unique bar code was being used more than once."

